Overlay patch 0045: THE MEMORY GOVERNOR (VR R8 part C).

WHAT KILLED THE USER'S SESSIONS, and it was never a crash. The 2026-09-05 pull
from the headset holds no signal record at all -- `Documents/crash.txt` was
still July's SIGABRT -- and one JetsamEvent: soh at rpages 299184 x 16 KB =
4.90 GB resident, lifetimeMax 5.05 GB, active and frontmost, system free
250 MB. That is a SIGKILL from the kernel's memory-pressure killer. No handler
runs, no backtrace exists, and no amount of better death-reporting would ever
have produced one.

THE SLOPE, from `vr-mem.log` (579 lines, 5 launches, 3385 s): available memory
falls 5082 -> 150 MB, and the rate is SCENE-DEPENDENT by more than ten times --
Hyrule Field (scene 81) burns 400-950 MB/min while Kokiri Forest (85) burns
16-52, and a 290 s flat-mode segment in the same scene grew by NOTHING. Both
scenes run at the same frame rate. A per-frame allocation leak cannot do that.
**The growth tracks new TEXTURE CONTENT, not frames.**

THE CAUSE. `ResourceManager::mResourceCache` is a strong `shared_ptr` map that
NOTHING ever evicts: `UnloadResource`'s only callers are SoH's alt-asset
toggles. Every distinct texture Link walks past leaves a permanently resident
decoded CPU copy for the life of the process -- on top of the GPU copy in
Fast3D's own cache. With a 4K texture pack installed (the user runs one) that is
gigabytes, it explains the scene dependence exactly, and it explains the part
that looked strangest in the log: the baseline RATCHETS while in VR and does
not come back when VR exits. VR is not the leak. VR is the ~800 MB of eye,
HUD and compositor surfaces that removes the headroom the flat build had.

THE FIX, in four parts, and the ORDER of the last two is load-bearing.

1. `ResourceManager::SohIosTrimTextureResources` -- evict cached resources of
   type OTEX only, and only those whose use_count says the cache is the last
   owner. TEXTURES ARE THE ONLY SAFE CLASS and that is not a guess: both OTR
   texture opcodes (`G_SETTIMG_OTR_HASH`, `G_SETTIMG_OTR_FILEPATH`) re-resolve
   their texture through the ResourceManager on EVERY execution of the display
   list, so an evicted texture is simply reloaded on the next draw. Vertices,
   skeletons, animations and object segments are handed to the game as RAW
   POINTERS that outlive the shared_ptr (`ResourceMgr_GetResourceDataByName-
   HandlingMQ` returns `GetRawPointer()` and drops its reference immediately),
   so evicting one of those would be a use-after-free. This patch will not
   touch them.
   The doomed resources are moved into a local vector, erased under the lock,
   and destroyed AFTER it is released -- a resource destructor can load other
   resources, which would deadlock on the mutex we are holding (upstream's own
   `UnloadResource` comment says so and then never actually does it).

2. `SohIos_TexCacheClearHard` -- Fast3D's texture cache is keyed by the CPU
   ADDRESS of the texture data. Freeing texture resources therefore makes the
   allocator free to hand the same address to a DIFFERENT texture, which would
   alias onto a live cache entry and draw the wrong image. So a resource trim
   is ALWAYS followed by a full texture-cache clear, and the clear releases the
   GPU textures for real: upstream's `TextureCacheClear` only parks the ids on
   a free list and never calls `DeleteTexture`, so it frees no memory at all.
   This is also why `gfx_texture_cache_clear()` having zero callers was not the
   whole story -- calling it would have freed nothing.

3. `SohIos_ServiceMemoryTrim(sceneNum)` -- serviced from `RunFrame`, on the
   GAME thread, at the top of a frame with no display list built and no
   interpreter frame open. Nothing may free a texture from another thread: the
   memory-pressure source runs on a dispatch queue and can only ever set a
   FLAG. The trim fires on request, on a scene change, and on a five-second
   timer while over `gSohIos.ResCacheBudgetMB` (default 768; 0 disables it
   outright, which is the A/B control). THE TIMER IS NOT OPTIONAL: Hyrule Field
   killed the app in 60-170 s WITHOUT leaving the scene, so a scene-change-only
   governor would have watched the user die. A trim costs a full texture
   re-upload, so it carries a 15 s cooldown, and a trim that frees less than
   32 MB backs off to a minute -- a cache that big because it is all in USE
   cannot be trimmed, and repeating it would buy nothing at real cost.

4. `SohIos_MetalDeviceRegister` / `SohIos_MetalAllocatedBytes` /
   `SohIos_PendingMipsCount` -- the measurement side. `MTLDevice
   currentAllocatedSize` splits GPU from CPU in one number, and the pending-mip
   count is the direct read of the H4 leak that overlay 0019's regeneration in
   this same round fixes.

WHY THE STATS WALK IS NOT PER FRAME. `SohIos_ResCacheStats` walks the whole
cache; at 10k entries that is tens of microseconds, fine at the 5 s heartbeat
and wasteful at 60 Hz. The service function only computes it when the scene
actually changed or a trim was requested.

